  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12a-2024-p8.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The equation means \\(\\sin(3\\theta)\\cos(2\\theta)=1\\) with both factors positive (for the logs to be defined). Since \\(\\sin(3\\theta)\\le1\\) and \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\le1,\\) their product is \\(1\\) only if \\(\\sin(3\\theta)=1\\) and \\(\\cos(2\\theta)=1\\) simultaneously. But \\(\\cos(2\\theta)=1\\) forces \\(\\theta\\in\\{0,\\pi,2\\pi\\},\\) where \\(\\sin(3\\theta)=0\\ne1.\\) No angle works. Thus, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "\\(\\log(\\sin3\\theta)+\\log(\\cos2\\theta)\\) \\(=\\log(\\sin3\\theta\\cos2\\theta),\\) which must equal \\(0\\)",
        "A product of two numbers each at most \\(1\\) equals \\(1\\) only when both equal \\(1\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
